WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.351
SDKs can improve consistency, but they do not eliminate responsibility.

2
00:00:04.351 --> 00:00:21.335
Official and third-party SDKs may handle authentication, signing,
pagination, retries, serialization, and endpoint construction, yet the
application still owns credential scope, business authorization, data
minimization, response handling, version pinning, and monitoring.

3
00:00:21.335 --> 00:00:32.775
Dependency review should consider package source, maintenance, transitive
dependencies, configuration defaults, deprecation plans, and how the SDK
exposes security-sensitive options.

4
00:00:32.775 --> 00:00:35.512
Testing should cover more than the happy path.

5
00:00:35.512 --> 00:00:54.180
API client tests should exercise authentication failure, authorization
failure, malformed responses, unexpected content types, oversized
responses, timeouts, connection failures, rate limits, retries, pagination
boundaries, duplicate records, redirects, unavailable services, and
contract changes.

6
00:00:54.180 --> 00:01:02.883
Mocked and staged API testing can both help, as long as tests do not teach
the code to trust unrealistic responses or ignore important failure modes.

7
00:01:02.883 --> 00:01:05.971
Logging must be useful without becoming a data leak.

8
00:01:05.971 --> 00:01:18.112
Outbound requests and responses may contain tokens, credentials, sensitive
headers, full URLs with query parameters, private payloads, regulated
data, and response bodies that should not be retained.

9
00:01:18.112 --> 00:01:27.165
Safe logging uses redaction, event names, correlation identifiers, timing,
destination identity, status categories, and high-impact audit events.

10
00:01:27.165 --> 00:01:33.762
The point is to support troubleshooting and accountability while limiting
exposure in long-lived log stores.

11
00:01:33.762 --> 00:01:37.271
Monitoring and incident response complete the lifecycle.

12
00:01:37.271 --> 00:01:49.343
Watch for unusual destinations, authentication failures, authorization
errors, retry storms, latency changes, quota exhaustion, error spikes,
unexpected redirects, and changes in response shape.

13
00:01:49.343 --> 00:01:59.800
If credentials, requests, or responses are exposed, teams need a path to
revoke or rotate credentials, assess data exposure, review logs, notify
owners, and improve controls.

14
00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:03.800
Secure API clients are maintained systems, not one-time snippets.
